<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>ndLook</string>
	<!--
	  What ndLook itself opens. This is what puts the app in the
	  Finder's "Open With" menu and lets a double-click route a file to
	  it; the UTI declarations below only teach the system what the types
	  *are*, not who can open them.

	  Every entry is a Viewer, never an Editor: ndLook reads all five
	  formats and writes none of them (NDLookDocument declares an empty
	  writableContentTypes).

	  LSHandlerRank splits along exactly the same line as the
	  imported/exported UTI split below. The three file formats are
	  Alternate -- ndLook is a useful opener for a .nc file but has no
	  business displacing whatever the user already opens netCDF with.
	  The two directory stores are Owner, because this project is their
	  owner of record and there is no other claimant to defer to.

	  These five entries and NDLookDocument.readableContentTypes must
	  stay in lockstep. This list is what the Finder will offer ndLook
	  for; that list is what the app will actually accept. A type in only
	  one of them yields either an "Open With" entry that opens a window
	  the app then refuses to fill, or a format the app could read but
	  that nothing in the Finder will hand it.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>NetCDF Dataset</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Alternate</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.netcdf</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>HDF5 Dataset</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Alternate</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.hdf5</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>GRIB Dataset</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Alternate</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.grib</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>Zarr Store</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Owner</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.zarr</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>Icechunk Repository</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Owner</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.icechunk</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.utilities</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>15.0</string>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  These three UTIs are minted by this project (there is no third-party
	  owner to "import" from), but they are declared as *imported* rather
	  than *exported* on purpose. UTExportedTypeDeclarations is for apps
	  that *own* a file format; UTImportedTypeDeclarations is for apps that
	  merely need to reference a type identifier. ndLook reads netCDF,
	  HDF5 and GRIB but does not define them, so importing is the honest
	  declaration -- the two directory stores below are the ones this
	  project actually owns.

	  Declaring them is what lets the PreviewExtension target match against
	  them in its QLSupportedContentTypes, and what gives the
	  CFBundleDocumentTypes entries above something to name.

	  Not owning these formats is also why those entries take LSHandlerRank
	  Alternate and why there are no UTTypeIcons here: ndLook offers
	  itself as an opener without claiming the file extension or the
	  document icon from whichever app the user already uses.
	-->
	<key>UTImportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.netcdf</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>NetCDF Dataset</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>nc</string>
					<string>nc4</string>
					<string>cdf</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.hdf5</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>HDF5 Dataset</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>h5</string>
					<string>hdf5</string>
					<string>he5</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.grib</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>GRIB Dataset</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>grib</string>
					<string>grib2</string>
					<string>grb</string>
					<string>grb2</string>
					<string>gb2</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Zarr stores and Icechunk repositories are *directories*, not files, and
	  no one else has minted a UTI for either. These two are therefore
	  declared as *exported*: this project defines the types, so it is their
	  owner of record.

	  Both conform to com.apple.package (a directory the Finder presents as a
	  single opaque item, which is what makes a `.zarr`/`.icechunk` folder
	  previewable at all) and to public.folder (so anything that reasons
	  about directories still treats them correctly). The tag specification
	  binds them to the conventional directory-name extensions; a store
	  without one of those extensions stays an ordinary folder to the system,
	  even though the Rust reader would happily preview it.

	  Because this app exports the types, the Finder takes their document
	  icons from it too. UTTypeIcons asks the system (macOS 11+) to compose
	  one: the standard document shape, the DocumentBadge image set from
	  Assets.xcassets (the app icon's cube without the loupe, see docs/icon)
	  as the centre badge, and a short label along the bottom edge. Keeping
	  the icons on the UTI declaration rather than on the matching
	  CFBundleDocumentTypes entries means one definition covers every store
	  the system meets, whether or not ndLook is what opens it. The icons
	  only appear once Launch Services has registered the app (install-dev.sh
	  does that); after changing them, re-register and relaunch the Finder,
	  which caches icons aggressively.

	  Owning these two types is also why their CFBundleDocumentTypes entries
	  take LSHandlerRank Owner rather than Alternate: there is no other
	  claimant to defer to, so ndLook is the default opener for a .zarr or
	  .icechunk directory.
	-->
	<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.zarr</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>Zarr Store</string>
			<key>UTTypeIcons</key>
			<dict>
				<key>UTTypeIconBadgeName</key>
				<string>DocumentBadge</string>
				<key>UTTypeIconText</key>
				<string>ZARR</string>
			</dict>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.apple.package</string>
				<string>public.folder</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>zarr</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.alexkerney.ndlook.icechunk</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>Icechunk Repository</string>
			<key>UTTypeIcons</key>
			<dict>
				<key>UTTypeIconBadgeName</key>
				<string>DocumentBadge</string>
				<key>UTTypeIconText</key>
				<string>ICECHUNK</string>
			</dict>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.apple.package</string>
				<string>public.folder</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>icechunk</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
